      "problem": "If $16z^2 + 49 = 0,$ then $z =$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we rearrange the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n16z^2 + 49 & = 0\\[5pt]\n\n16z^2 & = -49 \\[5pt]\n\nz^2 & = -\\dfrac{49}{16}\n}\n$$\n\nBy taking the square root, we obtain\n\n$$\nz = \\pm \\sqrt{-\\dfrac{49}{16}}.\n$$\n\nWe now rewrite $z$ using the imaginary unit $\\textrm{i}=\\sqrt{-1}\\, \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\pm \\sqrt{-\\frac{49}{16}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{49}{16}\\cdot (-1)} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{49}{16}}\\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{49}}{\\sqrt{16}}\\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{7}{4}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $z=\\pm \\dfrac{7}{4} \\textrm{i}.$",
